 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Watchdog function",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Each valid edge on WDI, rising or falling, clears the internal timer. WDO stays high while edges keep arriving inside tWD. If tWD runs out with no edge, WDO is driven low for tWDO, and after it returns high the timeout count starts again from zero. The part type and the three SET inputs fix the timing."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Startup delay",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "At power-up, and whenever a SET input changes, a startup delay runs before the timeout count begins; WDO does not pulse during it. On MAX6369/MAX6370 the startup delay equals the selected timeout, 1 ms to 60 s minimum. On MAX6371/MAX6372 it is fixed at 60 s while the timeout is selectable from 1 ms to 60 s. MAX6373/MAX6374 offer startup delays of 200 µs to 60 s and timeouts of 3 ms to 10 s in five settings, plus two settings with no timed startup: the timer waits for the first WDI edge however long that takes, for systems that need more than 60 s to initialise. WDI edges should start before the minimum startup delay ends, and then come faster than the minimum timeout, to avoid unwanted pulses."
  },
  {
   "topic": "WDI timing",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Timing counts from the last WDI edge belonging to a pulse at least 100 ns wide. Edges are disregarded while WDO is low, during the startup delay, and for a setup time tSETUP (300 µs at most) that follows power-up and every SET change."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Choosing and changing the timing",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "SET2, SET1 and SET0 may be tied to fixed levels or driven by logic and changed while running. Toggle WDI just before changing them, so a change late in a countdown does not cause a stray pulse. A change made while WDO is low lets the running pulse finish, then the new setting applies and, 300 µs of setup later, its startup delay begins. A change made while WDO is high takes effect at once, and its startup delay likewise begins once 300 µs of setup has passed."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Disable",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "SET2 = 0, SET1 = 1, SET0 = 1 turns the watchdog off on every part in the family, with WDO held high. Moving to any other setting turns it back on without cycling power: the part takes the new timing and enters startup once the 300 µs setup time has passed."
  },
  {
   "topic": "First-edge mode (MAX6373/MAX6374)",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "In settings 101 and 110 the startup period is not timed; it ends at the first WDI transition. To enter first-edge mode while the part is running, select the disable setting first and then the first-edge setting; this keeps WDO deasserted and prevents WDI from being masked."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "MAX6369/MAX6371/MAX6373: open-drain, active-low WDO with a 100 ms pulse, sinking current while low. A pullup goes from WDO to any supply up to +5.5 V; it must be large enough for the low level in the electrical table and small enough to give a valid high while feeding every input and leakage on the line. 10 kΩ suits most uses. MAX6370/MAX6372/MAX6374: push-pull, active-low WDO with a 1 ms pulse."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Noisy environments",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Where electrical noise is present, bypass VCC to GND with 0.1 µF placed close to the part, at most 0.2 in away."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Software",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Drive WDI high and low from separate places in the code instead of issuing a complete pulse in one spot, so a loop stuck in one place cannot keep the timer cleared. One scheme: set WDI high at the start of the main program, set it low as each subroutine or loop finishes, and set it high again on return to the start. If a subroutine hangs, WDI stays put, the timer expires and WDO pulses."
  }
 ],
